+Ordered CPU migration
+=====================
+
+An optional ordered mode supports policies which change the CPU of an active
+flow. It is independent of ``CONFIG_RPS``. The BPF program chooses the candidate
+CPU and an ordering domain; CPUMAP delays a change until the previous target has
+finished the domain's earlier submissions. A domain is a preallocated slot, not
+a kernel flow table. Packet parsing, socket association and CPU selection remain
+in BPF.
+
+Set ``map_extra`` to the number of domains when creating the CPUMAP. Zero keeps
+the ordinary CPUMAP behavior. Domains have indices from zero to
+``map_extra - 1`` and exist for the lifetime of the map. CPU entries retain their
+usual keys and ``struct bpf_cpumap_val`` values. The memory used by domains is
+allocated at map creation; allocation failure fails map creation.
+
+An ordered map accepts submissions only through ``bpf_cpumap_redirect()``.
+Calling ``bpf_redirect_map()`` on it returns ``XDP_ABORTED``. This prevents
+ordinary submissions from bypassing the ordered accounting in the same map.
+It does not prevent a BPF program from passing a packet locally or using a
+different map.
+
+Kernel BPF interface
+--------------------
+
+The following kfuncs are available to XDP programs. As with other kfuncs, their
+interfaces are not a stable userspace ABI. Include ``vmlinux.h`` for the state
+type and declare the kfuncs with ``__ksym``::
+
+    extern int bpf_cpumap_redirect(void *map, __u32 cpu,
+                                  __u32 domain) __ksym;
+    extern int bpf_cpumap_get_state(void *map, __u32 domain,
+                                   void *state, __u32 size) __ksym;
+
+``map`` must be a constant map pointer known to the verifier.
+
+``bpf_cpumap_redirect()`` returns ``XDP_REDIRECT`` for an ordered CPUMAP with
+valid CPU and domain indices, or ``XDP_ABORTED`` for invalid arguments. It
+records a redirect request, like ``bpf_redirect_map()``. The program must
+return ``XDP_REDIRECT`` to submit the packet. Returning another action, or
+replacing the request with another redirect, leaves no reserved submission.
+A successful kfunc return does not guarantee queue admission.
+
+At actual submission, CPUMAP selects the candidate if the domain is idle.
+Otherwise it keeps the previous target. An absent or offline candidate also
+keeps a usable previous target. If no usable target exists, the redirect fails
+and the packet is dropped through the existing redirect error path. Queue
+exhaustion drops the packet without leaving an outstanding completion ticket.
+These failures do not create a local ``XDP_PASS`` fallback: such a packet could
+overtake traffic still running on the previous CPU.
+
+``bpf_cpumap_get_state()`` copies the following snapshot into an initialized
+buffer of exactly ``sizeof(struct bpf_cpumap_state)`` bytes::
+
+    struct bpf_cpumap_state {
+        __u64 last_tail;
+        __u64 completed;
+        __u32 cpu;
+        __u32 pending;
+    };
+
+It returns zero on success and ``-EINVAL`` for an unsuitable map, domain or
+buffer size. An unused domain has ``cpu == ~0U`` and zero counters. Otherwise:
+
+* ``cpu`` identifies the retained target entry, including a retired entry.
+* ``pending`` counts source reservations which have not yet been published
+  to the target ring or dropped. Native XDP source bulks can hold these.
+* ``last_tail`` identifies the domain's last successful ring insertion.
+* ``completed`` is that entry's completed prefix, shared by all its domains.
+
+The counters are unsigned, wrapping 64-bit sequence numbers. After stopping
+new producers, a domain has drained when ``pending == 0`` and
+``(__s64)(completed - last_tail) >= 0``. Like other sequence-counter
+comparisons, this assumes fewer than half the sequence space is outstanding.
+The snapshot is informational while producers run; observing an idle domain
+does not reserve it or authorize a concurrent teardown. Ticket values from
+different entries, maps or snapshots must not be combined.
+
+Ordering and lifetime
+---------------------
+
+The kernel reserves a submission before leaving the domain lock. Pending
+source bulks keep the target fixed. Successful ring insertions receive tickets
+under the ring's producer lock. Failed insertions return the reservation and
+do not allocate a ticket. The consumer preserves ring order for native frames
+and generic XDP skbs, including batches containing both.
+
+Completion is published after a full GRO flush and synchronous handoff of the
+normal receive list to the network stack, every 64 packets or when the ring
+becomes empty. It is not published merely because a frame was dequeued or
+because the remote XDP program returned ``XDP_PASS``. Remote drops and skb
+allocation failures also retire their tickets. The entry's remote XDP program
+may pass or drop packets; a second ``XDP_REDIRECT`` is dropped because its
+asynchronous completion cannot be tracked by this map.
+
+Replacing or deleting a CPU entry first removes it from the map, then drains
+its existing source bulks and ring through the usual RCU/kthread lifecycle.
+Domains retain references to its progress until they can switch. They do not
+submit new packets to a retired entry: a busy domain can temporarily drop
+packets during replacement. Recreating an entry at the same CPU index does
+not make it the same target generation.
+
+As in native RFS, an offline previous CPU is an exception to waiting for its
+completion watermark. Pending source submissions must still be accounted
+before switching. CPUMAP retains its existing kthread/hotplug behavior;
+this is not a guarantee of ordering or lossless delivery across CPU hotplug.
+The scheduler can relax an offlined worker's affinity. After bringing its CPU
+online again, the control plane must replace that CPU entry before relying on
+the original worker affinity. The normal entry replacement barrier applies.
+
+The guarantee concerns CPU migration of submissions to the same domain.
+It cannot reconstruct wire order when multiple RX queues or producers already
+present a flow out of order, and it does not serialize unrelated flows which
+GRO may deliver independently. Native RPS or another asynchronous stage after
+CPUMAP requires its own ordering analysis; disable native RPS on this receive
+path when using CPUMAP as its sole software CPU steering mechanism.
+
+Policy and control-plane responsibilities
+----------------------------------------
+
+All packets of an active flow must use the same map and domain, including
+packets before a socket is available. A BPF flow table can store domain IDs;
+collisions may conservatively delay migration of otherwise unrelated flows.
+Changing a flow's domain in flight defeats the migration barrier.
+
+XDP socket lookup and ``bpf_sk_storage_get()`` can read socket policy state
+recorded by socket or tracing BPF programs. The lookup reference must be
+released with ``bpf_sk_release()``. This storage can supply the candidate CPU,
+but it does not replace the pre-socket classification/domain needed for an
+active flow. Socket lookup misses, fragments, non-local packets and namespace
+or tunnel boundaries need explicit policy decisions. The infrastructure
+neither chooses a fallback CPU nor parses these packet formats.
+
+Before reassigning a domain to a different identity, replacing the entire map,
+switching an active flow to local PASS, or detaching the ingress program,
+quiesce the affected ingress and drain its domains. Prevent new submissions
+throughout the transition. Merely reading an idle snapshot while traffic
+continues, closing a socket or deleting a policy-map entry is insufficient.
+Live atomic transitions between these paths are not provided by this API.
+
+Cost
+----
+
+Ordered maps add domain locking, ring ticket accounting, retained progress
+references, and completion publication. Their source bulks also store domain
+pointers. No per-packet accounting object is allocated. Full GRO flushes at
+the completion boundary can reduce aggregation compared to ordinary CPUMAP's
+age-based flushes under sustained load. Benchmark throughput, latency and GRO
+aggregation on the intended NIC, packet mix and queue configuration.
+
+Ordinary maps do not allocate domains or progress state and keep their existing
+redirect and remote-program semantics. The shared paths contain additional
+mode checks; no zero-overhead claim is made for these checks.
+
